    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nAdded a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the Owner resource that is `true` when the owner's city already held **40\u201349 owners** at creation time (approaching the capacity limit of 50). The hard rejection at 50 is unchanged.\n\n## Files touched\n\n1. **`ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 In the owner-create path I now compute the city count once, keep the existing hard rejection (`>= 50` \u2192 `CityOwnerLimitExceededException`), and set the new flag when the count is in the `40\u201349` band. Added a `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40L` constant mirroring the existing `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD` style.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the persisted `capacityWarning` field with `isCapacityWarning()` / `setCapacityWarning()`, following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` / `possibleDuplicate` advisory-flag pattern.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` schema property (boolean, `readOnly`) to the Owner model, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it (same as the other warning flags).\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe approach matches the codebase's convention: the warning is evaluated against the pre-create city count and persisted on the owner, then surfaced via the derived DTO field \u2014 exactly how `bulkSignupWarning` works. `AcceptanceTest.coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` (fill a city to 40, then the 41st owner warns) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` with no failures.\n\n(No commit made \u2014 this directory isn't a git repository.)",
